#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# norm

#% text
# `norm(v)` gives the Euclidean length of the vector `v`.

#% text
# `norm(v, p)` gives the `p`-norm of `v`, the `p`-th root of the sum of `abs(v[i])^p`, for a positive integer `p` or `inf`.

#% text
# `norm(m)` gives the 2-norm of the matrix `m`, its largest singular value.

#% text
# `norm(m, p)` gives the 1-norm, the infinity norm or the Frobenius norm of `m` for `p` equal to 1, `inf` or `"frobenius"`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `norm(v, inf)` is the largest absolute value of an entry.

#% item
# The matrix 1-norm is the largest column sum of absolute values; the infinity norm is the largest row sum.

#% item
# The Frobenius norm is the square root of the sum of the squares of the absolute values of all entries.

#% item
# Complex entries are allowed. Exact entries give exact norms, machine and ball entries numerical ones.

#% item
# Any other `p` gives a failure.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
norm([3, 4])
#% output
#= 5

#% cell
norm([1, 2, 3])
#% output
#= 14^(1/2)

#% text
# The 2-norm of a matrix:

#% cell
norm([[1, 2], [3, 4]])
#% output
#= (15 + 221^(1/2))^(1/2)

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Other vector norms:

#% cell
norm([1, -2, 3], 1)
#% output
#= 6

#% cell
norm([1, -2, 3], inf)
#% output
#= 3

#% cell
norm([1, 2, 3], 3)
#% output
#= 6^(2/3)

#% text
# Complex and symbolic entries:

#% cell
norm([3 + 4im, 0])
#% output
#= 5

#% cell
norm([a, b])
#% output
#= (abs(a)^2 + abs(b)^2)^(1/2)

#% text
# Machine numbers and balls:

#% cell
norm([[1.0, 2.0], [3.0, 4.0]])
#% output
#= 5.4649857042190435

#% cell
norm(n([1, 1], 30))
#% output
#= 1.4142135623730950488016887242096981

#% subsection
# Options

#% text
# Matrix norms:

#% cell
norm([[1, 2], [3, 4]], 1)
#% output
#= 6

#% cell
norm([[1, 2], [3, 4]], inf)
#% output
#= 7

#% cell
norm([[1, 2], [3, 4]], "frobenius")
#% output
#= 30^(1/2)

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# A unit vector in the direction of `v`:

#% cell
v = [1, 2, 2]; v/norm(v)
#% output
#= [1/3, 2/3, 2/3]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The 2-norm of a matrix is its largest singular value:

#% cell
n(norm([[1, 2], [3, 4]]))
#% output
#= 5.464985704219043

#% cell
singular_values([[1.0, 2.0], [3.0, 4.0]])[1]
#% output
#= 5.4649857042190435

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# The order must be a positive integer or `inf`:

#% cell
norm([1, 1], 2.5)
#% output
#! failure(argument, norm, 2)

#% text
# A number is not a vector:

#% cell
norm(-5)
#% output
#= norm(-5)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `dot`, `abs`, `singular_values`, `cross`
